Take the non-ringbuffer work off the acquisition host. This is the fix for the
45% sample loss, and it is a subtraction rather than a tuning flag.

**The measurement.** On 2026-08-12, killing only the processes that do *not*
read the ringbuffer -- the five digisonde receivers and the three plotters --
while `detect_chirps` and `calc_ionograms` kept running:

    digisonde: 0  plotters: 0
    load average: 9.15 -> 6.98
    0 -> 0 samp_diff events in 600 s      (was ~1500/s)

Zero, over ten minutes. `tail` on `thor.log` showed an unbroken run of progress
dots with no `D` markers at all.

**Why these and not others.** Split the station's processes by one question:
does it read the DigitalRF ringbuffer? Anything that does is bound to
`/dev/shm` at 25 MS/s and can never leave this machine -- relocating it would
mean shipping 100 MB/s, 8.6 TB/day, across the network:

    rx_uhd_ext_gps        0.8 cores
    drf ringbuffer        0.5
    detect_chirps  x2     1.9
    calc_ionograms x2     1.2
                        -----
                          4.4 of 8

Anything that does not is free to run anywhere. 4.4 cores of unavoidable work
on an eight-core host has room to spare; 7.3 does not.

**The digisonde receivers are the striking case.** They download from GIRO over
HTTP. They have no connection to the radio at all, yet five of them were eating
two cores on the one machine in the building that must not miss a packet.
Because their input arrives over the internet and never touches local data,
they can run anywhere with network access and write **straight to the NAS**.
The archive tree ends up identical -- `digisonde_ionogram-*.h5` are real
products and the MUF pipeline compares them against the chirp ionograms; only
the machine that writes them changes. No export of the laptop's disk is needed,
which keeps this host a pure source.

**The plotters are deleted, not moved.** The web UI renders ionograms on
demand, so `plot_rtf.py`, `plot_detectionfiles.py` and `plot_ionograms.py` have
no consumer. Writing PNGs here costs ~0.7 cores to produce files nothing reads,
and a second rendering path is a second thing to keep consistent with the UI.

**Deliberately left alone.** `sync_iono_data.py`, `iono_housekeeping.py` and
`detections2metadata.py` (lines 99-106) stay for now. Two of them may duplicate
services this repo already provides -- `sync_iono_data.py` copies to the NAS
and so does `chirp-archive-sync.service`; anything that deletes on age alone
would race `chirp-archive-prune.service`, which only unlinks what it has
verified byte-for-byte on the remote. Two independent deleters on one tree is
how an archive loses what it cannot get back. Resolve that before moving them.

Note that `iono_housekeeping.py` was not present in the station's clone at all
on 2026-08-12: line 103 launches a missing file, so it has been dying instantly
into `logs/housekeeping.log`. Harmless, but it means no housekeeping has ever
run here.

**What this does not do.** Core isolation was the first instinct -- pin the
recorder and the NIC IRQ to cores nothing else may use, via `CPUAffinity` in
the systemd units. It was never needed. Relocation removes the contention
instead of drawing a fence around it, and needs no tuning to maintain. If drops
return after this, revisit isolation; do not add it pre-emptively.

**Verifying.** Wait at least ten minutes -- `thor.log` is block-buffered and an
early reading shows an unflushed buffer, not a healthy recorder. Use the rate
between two late samples, never a cumulative figure, and always print `uptime`
beside it:

    A=$(grep -c samp_diff logs/thor.log); sleep 600
    B=$(grep -c samp_diff logs/thor.log); echo "$(( (B-A)/600 )) events/s"; uptime

If the load average has not moved, the change did not take effect and the drop
figure means nothing -- that mistake has been made twice on this station.

Apply on top of 0004 and 0005:

    cd ~/chirpsounder2
    git apply --check /path/to/0007-dombas-move-digisonde-and-drop-plotters.patch
    git apply         /path/to/0007-dombas-move-digisonde-and-drop-plotters.patch

Takes effect on the next full `dombas.sh` launch, not on the recorder's 24-hour
restart -- that loop only relaunches `rx_uhd_ext_gps`. The processes removed
here are started once at script start, so a running station keeps them until
`dombas.sh` itself is restarted.

